#
# Run the flag-surface guards before a push reaches a shared branch.
#
# # Why a hook, when CI already runs this
#
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml`'s `cargo test --workspace` covers these tests, and
# it has never been the problem. The problem is *when* it runs: this repository
# is worked in dozens of local worktrees whose branches are merged into `dev`
# and pushed directly, so CI is a post-hoc detector — it can report red, it
# cannot prevent it. Four undeclared `CRATONVM_*` flags reached `dev` on
# 2026-08-11 alone, each found downstream by whoever branched next.
#
# The window this closes is exactly the one CI cannot: between "the change is
# committed" and "the change is on a branch someone else builds from".
#
# # Why these tests and not more
#
# Everything here is cheap and deterministic — no VM boots, no fixtures, no
# network. Warm, the three test targets take about 1.8 s together, which is the
# whole reason this is viable as a push-time gate. A hook that costs minutes
# gets `--no-verify`d permanently and is worse than no hook, so the scope is
# deliberately the flag surface and nothing else. It is not a substitute for
# running the tests your change actually touches.
#
# # Escape hatches, in order of preference
#
#   git push --no-verify              # skip every hook, once
#   CRATONVM_SKIP_FLAG_HOOK=1 git push  # skip just this check, once
#
# The second exists so a push can skip *this* gate without also skipping any
# other hook a future change adds.
#
# # Install
#
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# `core.hooksPath` is local configuration and cannot be committed, so this is
# opt-in per clone by design — see docs/contributing/flag-surface-hook.md.
set -uo pipefail

if [ -n "${CRATONVM_SKIP_FLAG_HOOK:-}" ]; then
  echo "pre-push: flag-surface guard skipped (CRATONVM_SKIP_FLAG_HOOK)" >&2
  exit 0
fi

# A delete-only push (`git push origin --delete <branch>`) has nothing to
# check: git feeds the hook a local ref of all zeros for each deletion. Reading
# stdin also matters for its own sake — a hook that leaves it unread can make
# git report a broken pipe on large pushes.
zero='0000000000000000000000000000000000000000'
have_update=0
while read -r _local_ref local_oid _remote_ref _remote_oid; do
  if [ "$local_oid" != "$zero" ]; then
    have_update=1
  fi
done
if [ "$have_update" -eq 0 ]; then
  exit 0
fi

# Resolve the repository even when the hook runs from a worktree or a
# subdirectory: `--show-toplevel` is the working tree's root, which is where
# the Cargo workspace lives.
ROOT="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
cd "$ROOT" || exit 1

CARGO_BIN="${CARGO:-$(command -v cargo 2>/dev/null || true)}"
if [ -z "$CARGO_BIN" ] && [ -x "$HOME/.cargo/bin/cargo" ]; then
  CARGO_BIN="$HOME/.cargo/bin/cargo"
fi
if [ -z "$CARGO_BIN" ]; then
  # Refusing to push because cargo is missing would be worse than the problem:
  # a docs-only push from a machine without a toolchain is legitimate. Say so
  # rather than passing silently, which would read as "checked, fine".
  echo "pre-push: cargo not found — flag-surface guard NOT run" >&2
  exit 0
fi

# Two invocations, not one. The three integration targets check the surface
# against the code and the docs; `flag_groups`'s own unit tests check the
# INVENTORY table's internal invariants - that a row expands to something, that
# `off_word` only appears on a default-ON knob with no opt-out key, and that no
# legacy variable is claimed by two tokens.
#
# Those live in the types crate's LIB, so the target list below never ran them,
# and the class went uncaught: on 2026-09-04 three `CRATONVM_XT_*` rows named
# the same variable as BOTH `on_key` and `off_key` and reached `dev`, holding
# two lib tests red until someone tripped over them downstream.
#
# Folding `--lib` into the one invocation would run the WHOLE types lib -
# measured 24 s against the ~2 s the three targets cost, which is exactly the
# number that gets a hook `--no-verify`d permanently. Filtered to `flag_groups`
# in its own invocation it is ~1 s warm.
echo "pre-push: checking the CRATONVM_* flag surface..." >&2
if "$CARGO_BIN" test -q -p cratonvm-types \
     --test flag_declaration_guard \
     --test flag_docs_generated \
     --test flag_surface \
   && "$CARGO_BIN" test -q -p cratonvm-types --lib flag_groups; then
  exit 0
fi

cat >&2 <<'EOF'

pre-push: the CRATONVM_* flag surface is out of step with the code.

An undeclared flag is served by a live `getenv` rather than by the latched
`VmFlags` snapshot, so `CRATONVM_<GROUP>=token` cannot reach it and
`flags::with_thread_overrides` cannot arrange it in a test — a flag-dependent
test then silently measures whatever the developer's environment happens to
say. That is why this is a gate and not a lint.

To fix, in order:

  1. types/src/flag_groups.rs   — add an `E { .. }` row to INVENTORY.
                                  Token stated positively, lower-case,
                                  hyphenated. A knob spelled only
                                  `CRATONVM_X_NO_Y` gets `off_key` and no
                                  `on_key`.
  2. the read site              — if it uses `std::env::var`, move it onto the
                                  crate's snapshot accessor (`nbflags()`,
                                  `gc_flags()`, ...) and add the typed field to
                                  types/src/flags.rs. If it already uses
                                  `flags::runtime_var[_os]`, step 1 is the
                                  whole fix: that function serves declared
                                  names from the snapshot on its own.
  3. types/tests/flag-surface.txt — add the name, keeping the file sorted.
  4. regenerate BOTH docs, do not hand-edit them:
         python tools/flag-census/render-inventory.py .
         bash   tools/flag-census/render-tokens.sh
     (render-inventory refuses to run if step 3 was skipped.)

Then re-run:

  cargo test -p cratonvm-types

If this push genuinely should not be gated on it:

  CRATONVM_SKIP_FLAG_HOOK=1 git push ...     # skip this check
  git push --no-verify ...                   # skip every hook

EOF
exit 1
